WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.240
What effect, if any, does a
vegetarian or plant-based diet

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.240
have on athletic performance?

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.527
Watch the video to find out.

00:00:09.527 --> 00:00:11.527
"The Gladiator Diet and the
First Studies on Vegetarian Athletes"

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.400
Recently, the remains of
dozens of Roman gladiators

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.560
were discovered in a mass grave.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.760
The clue to their identities
were the rather distinct types

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.080
of mortal injuries they found,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.480
like being speared in
the head with a trident.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.560
Using just their skeletons
they were able to reconstruct

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.560
the death blows, show just
how buff they really were,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.920
and even try to reconstruct
their diet of barley and beans.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.360
You can look at carbon isotopes and
see what kinds of plants they ate;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.240
nitrogen isotopes reflect any
intake of animal protein.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.800
You can also look at the
Sulphur in their bones

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.080
and the amount of strontium,
leading commentators to submit

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.920
that the best athletes
in ancient Rome ate

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.240
largely plant-based diets.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.920
Then there were the legionnaires,
the Roman army troopers,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.600
famed for their abilities, also
eating a similar kind of diet,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.840
suggesting “the best fighters
in the ancient world

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.720
were essentially vegetarian.”

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.400
So, if the so-called
perfect fighting machines,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.560
the great sports heroes of the day,
were eating mostly grains and beans,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.520
should that tell us anything
about sports nutrition

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.480
and the preferred diets
of elite athletes?

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.640
Well, most of the Greeks and
Romans were basically vegetarian,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.000
centering their diets around
grains, fruit, vegetables and beans,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.840
so maybe the gladiators’ diets
weren’t that remarkable.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.080
Plato, for example, pushed
plants, preferring plant foods

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.920
for their health and efficiency.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.560
So yes, the Roman gladiators
were known as the ‘‘barley men,’’

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.320
but is that because barley
gives you strength and stamina,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.760
or was that just the basic food
that people ate at the time,

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.760
not necessarily for performance,
but because it was just so cheap?

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.680
Well, if you look at the modern
Spartans, the Tarahumara Indians,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.320
the ones that run races where
they kick a ball for oh,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.720
75 miles just for the fun of it,
running all day, all night,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.640
and all day, maybe 150 miles
if they’re feeling in the mood.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.120
What do you get if you win?

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.160
A special popularity with the ladies
(although how much of a reward

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.640
that would actually prove to be
for a man who had been running

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.160
for two days straight is questionable;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.680
though, maybe their endurance
extends to other dimensions).

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.080
“Probably not since the
days of the ancient Spartans

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.560
has a people achieved such a high
state of extreme physical conditioning.”

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.480
And what did they eat?

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.520
The same kind of 75 to
80 percent starch diet

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.840
based on beans, corn, and squash.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.320
And, they had the cholesterol
levels to prove it,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.960
total cholesterol levels down at an
essentially heart attack proof 136.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.240
And it’s not just some
special genetics they have—

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.800
you feed them enough egg yolks and
their cholesterol creeps right up.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.840
Modern day Olympian runners
eat the same stuff.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.680
What are they eating over there in Kenya?

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.320
A 99 percent vegetarian diet centered
mostly around various starches.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.800
But as in all these cases, is
their remarkable physical prowess

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.000
because of their diets, or
in spite of their diets?

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.920
Or have nothing to do with their diets?

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.840
You don’t know…until
you put it to the test.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.000
In spite of well-documented health
benefits of more plant-based diets,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.840
less is known regarding the effects
of these diets on athletic performance.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.320
So, they compared elite vegetarian
and omnivore endurance athletes

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.240
for aerobic fitness and strength.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.720
So, comparing oxygen
utilization on the treadmill,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.520
and quad strength with leg extensions.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.800
And the vegetarians beat out
their omnivore counterparts

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.640
for cardiorespiratory fitness,
but their strength didn’t differ.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.440
Suggesting, in the very least,
that vegetarian diets

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.440
don’t compromise athletic performance.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.560
But this was a cross-sectional study.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.040
Maybe the veg athletes were just
fitter because they trained harder?

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.520
Like in the National Runners' Health Study

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.360
looking at thousands of runners:
vegetarian runners were recorded

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.480
running significantly
more on a weekly basis;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.040
so, maybe that explains
their superior fitness.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.080
Though, maybe their superior fitness
explains their greater distances.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.120
Other cross-sectional studies
have found no differences

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.720
in physical fitness between
vegetarian and non-vegetarian athletes,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.800
or even worse performance, as in this
study of vegetarian athletes in India.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.280
Of course, there could be socioeconomic
or other confounding factors.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.200
That’s why we need interventional
studies to put different diets

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.480
to the test and then compare
physical performance,

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.960
which we’ll explore next.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.480
In 1896, the aptly named
James Parsley evidently led

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.920
a successful vegetarian
cycling club to victory,

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.760
their competitors evidently having
to "eat crow with their beef."

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.000
Evidently some Belgian
put it to the test in 1904,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.880
with those eating more plant-based
supposedly lifting some weight

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.560
like 80 percent more times,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.920
but I couldn't find the
primary source in English.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.640
This I could find though: a famous
series of experiments at Yale,

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.480
published more than a century ago, on the
influence of flesh-eating on endurance.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.120
Forty-nine people were compared:
regular athletes (mostly Yale students),

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.040
vegetarian athletes, and then
just sedentary vegetarians.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.080
"The experiment furnished a severe test
of the claims of those flesh-abstainers."

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:50.480
Much to the researchers' surprise,
the results seemed to vindicate

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.600
the vegetarians, suggesting that
not eating meat leads to far greater

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.320
endurance compared to those accustomed
to the ordinary American diet.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.960
Check it out: the first endurance test was
how many minutes straight you could

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.880
hold out your arms horizontally:
flesh-eaters versus flesh abstainers.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.960
The regular Yale athletes were
able to keep their hands out

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.800
for about 10 minutes on average.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:20.240
It's harder than it sounds;
give it a try...

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.240
OK, but those eating vegetarian
did like five times better.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:34.080
The meat-eater maximum was only
half that of the vegetarian average.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.040
Only two meat eaters
even hit 15 minutes,

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.520
whereas more than two-thirds
of the meat-avoiders did.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.880
None of the regular diet
folks hit a half hour,

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.600
whereas nearly half of
the healthier eaters did,

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.280
including nine that exceeded an
hour, four that exceeded two hours,

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.800
and one guy going for
more than three hours.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.360
How many deep knee
bends can you do?

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.360
One athlete could do more
than 1,000—averaging 383—

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.400
but they got creamed even
by the sedentary plant-eaters.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:11.280
That's the crazy thing; even
the sedentary abstainers

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.560
surpassed the exercising flesh-eaters.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.240
The sedentary abstainers were,

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.880
in most cases, physicians
who sat on their butts all day.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.480
I want a doctor that that can do
a thousand deep knee bends!

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.080
And then in terms of recovery, all those
deep knee bends left everyone sore,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.560
but more so among those eating meat.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.640
Among the vegetarians, of two that
did like 2,000 knee bends, one went

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:39.040
straight off to the track to run and
another went on to their nursing duties.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.880
On the other hand, among the
meat-eaters one guy reached 254,

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.960
went down once more and couldn't
get back up, had to be carried away,

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:52.960
and was incapacitated for days; another
impaired for weeks after fainting.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.440
It may be inferred without reasonable
doubt, concluded the once skeptical

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.640
Yale researcher, that the meat-eating
group of athletes was very far inferior

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.160
in endurance to the vegetarians,
even the sedentary ones.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.480
What could account for
this remarkable difference?

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.280
Some claimed that flesh foods contained
some kind of "fatigue poisons,"

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.440
but one German researcher who detailed
his own experiments with athletes

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.520
offered a more prosaic answer.
In his book on what looks like

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.040
physiological studies of
uber-driving vegetarians—

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.120
I told you I only know English—

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.080
he conjectured that the apparent
vegetarian superiority was just due

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.120
to their tremendous determination
to prove their point

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.440
and spread their propaganda,
so they just make a greater effort

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.240
in any contest than do
their meat-eating rivals.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.400
The Yale researchers were worried
about this, and so special pains

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.400
were taken to stimulate the flesh-eaters
to the utmost, appealing to

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:57.840
their college pride. Don't let those lousy
vegetarians beat the "Yale spirit."

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.400
The experiments made it
into The New York Times.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:06.080
Yale's flesh-eating athletes—
sounds like a zombie movie—

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.240
beaten in severe endurance tests.
Yale professor believes that

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.400
he has shown definitely the inferiority
in strength and endurance tests

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.200
of meat eaters compared to
those who do not eat meat.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.360
Some of Yale's most successful
athletes took part in the strength tests,

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.160
and Professor Fisher declares they
were obliged to admit their inferiority.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.360
How has the truth of this result
been so long obscured?

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.400
One reason, Professor Fisher
suggested, is that vegetarians

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.120
are their own worst enemy.
In their fanaticism, they jump

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.800
from the premise that meat eating
is wrong—often based on scripture

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.000
or some kind of dogma—and jump
from that to meat-eating is unhealthy.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:57.040
That's not how science works
and such logical leaps get them

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:01.840
dismissed as zealots and prevent
any genuine scientific investigation.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.920
Lots of science, even back then,
was pointing a distinct trend

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.080
towards more plant-based eating,
and yet the word vegetarian—

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.200
even 110 years ago—had
such a bad, preachy rap

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.720
that many were loath to concede
the science in its favor.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.080
The proper scientific attitude is to study
the question of meat-eating in precisely

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:31.840
the same manner as one would
study the question of anything else.

